MongoDB's docs say a BSON Date "is a 64-bit integer that represents the number of milliseconds since the Unix epoch". It is signed, so dates before 1970 work. It stores no zone. If you need the user's zone, put it in its own field:
db.meetings.insertOne({
title: "Standup",
startsAt: ISODate("2026-12-07T09:00:00Z"), // instant, for queries and sorting
localTime: "09:00", // what the user chose
tz: "Europe/London" // how to recompute it
});
See MongoDB Course 00 · Foundations for BSON types, and MongoDB Course 28 · Time-Series for time-based data at scale.

Java 8 added java.time, with one class per concept (see Java Development p1 and p2 for the language basics):
import java.time.*;
public class TimeDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Instant now = Instant.now(); // an instant
LocalDate due = LocalDate.of(2026, 5, 21); // a calendar date
ZoneId ny = ZoneId.of("America/New_York");
ZonedDateTime gap = ZonedDateTime.of(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30, 0, 0, ny);
System.out.println(gap); // 2026-03-08T03:30-04:00[America/New_York]
ZonedDateTime overlap = ZonedDateTime.of(2026, 11, 1, 1, 30, 0, 0, ny);
System.out.println(overlap); // ...T01:30-04:00[...]
System.out.println(overlap.withLaterOffsetAtOverlap()); // ...T01:30-05:00[...]
ZonedDateTime hk = LocalDateTime.of(2026, 5, 21, 15, 0)
.atZone(ZoneId.of("Asia/Hong_Kong"));
System.out.println(hk.toInstant()); // 2026-05-21T07:00:00Z
}
}
The ZonedDateTime docs give the rules: in a gap the local time is "shifted forwards by the length of the Gap"; in an overlap the earlier offset is used unless you call withLaterOffsetAtOverlap().

The port corrects this. Its loader reads the wall-clock fields back out of that Date and anchors them to Hong Kong time with a fixed offset. Simplified from the source:
const TZ_OFFSET_HOURS = 8; // Hexo blog built in Asia/Hong_Kong.
const pad = (n: number) => String(n).padStart(2, "0");
function parseFrontmatterDate(v: Date): Date {
const y = v.getUTCFullYear(), mo = v.getUTCMonth(), d = v.getUTCDate();
const h = v.getUTCHours(), mi = v.getUTCMinutes(), s = v.getUTCSeconds();
return new Date(Date.UTC(y, mo, d, h - TZ_OFFSET_HOURS, mi, s));
}
function postPath(date: Date, slug: string) {
const d = new Date(date.getTime() + TZ_OFFSET_HOURS * 3600_000); // shift to HKT
return `${d.getUTCFullYear()}/${pad(d.getUTCMonth() + 1)}/${pad(d.getUTCDate())}/${slug}/`;
}
Result: 2026-05-21T07:00:00Z (15:00 HKT), and the URL /2026/05/21/slug/ uses the Hong Kong calendar date. Date.UTC accepts an hour below 0 and rolls back to the previous day, so a post at 03:00 HKT still gets the right instant. A fixed +8 is safe here only because Hong Kong has had no DST since 1979. The same trick would be wrong for London.
